  "assets": [
    {
      "assetId": "math.geom.pythagorean-converse/learning_objective/1",
      "kind": "learning_objective",
      "conceptId": "math.geom.pythagorean-converse",
      "title": "Learning Objectives",
      "content": "- LO1: Apply the CONVERSE of the Pythagorean theorem — given a triangle's three side lengths $a,b,c$ (with $c$ the LONGEST), if $a^2+b^2=c^2$, then the triangle IS right-angled, with the right angle OPPOSITE the longest side $c$.\n- LO2: Recognize that the converse is a genuinely SEPARATE logical statement from the original theorem — the original says \"right triangle $\\Rightarrow$ $a^2+b^2=c^2$\"; the converse says \"$a^2+b^2=c^2$ $\\Rightarrow$ right triangle\" — and while BOTH happen to be true here, a theorem's converse is not automatically true in general (this is a special, provable fact about THIS particular theorem).\n- LO3: Correctly identify which side must be $c$ (the LONGEST) before testing the equation — testing with the WRONG side as $c$ produces a false negative (incorrectly concluding \"not a right triangle\" when it actually is one).",
      "localKey": null,
      "language": "en",
